    <encodingDesc>
      <projectDesc>
        <p>One colophon per TEI file. Three witness divisions preserve complete separate transcriptions, each with its own mise-en-page description and folio blocks. Paris, Bibliothèque nationale de France, MS. Arménien 178 is the base surviving copy. No reconstructed original or critical apparatus is supplied.</p>
      </projectDesc>
      <editorialDecl>
        <p>Keep the source transcription separate from the translation and editorial
          commentary. Adopted policies for punctuation, abbreviation expansion, and normalisation
          are recorded in editorial standard doc which will be published alongside final populated edition. 
          Editorial intervention must remain visible.</p>
        <p>For each folio division, use ab type="transcription" xml:lang="xcl" and ab type="translation" xml:lang="en". Translation corresp points to the Armenian block.</p>
        <p>Each folio division identifies the page and links to its facsimile. Record column changes with cb and line beginnings with lb. English cb/lb retain the supplied editorial alignment. Keep n only every five lines, restarting in each column; no routine line IDs.</p>
        <p>Describe mise en page for this witness and relevant folios: placement, relationship
          to surrounding text, columns, spacing, indentation, headings, rubrication,
          decoration, and relevant script or hand contrasts. A later copy may have different layout from the colophon's original inscription.</p>
        <p>Encode passage-specific commentary inline using an anchor type="commentary" with a short xml:id before the explained passage, followed by note type="commentary" with corresp pointing to that anchor.</p>
        <p>Armenian formula seg/@ana links directly to standard-pattern definitions in listformula.xml. Formula classifications follow authority/listformula.xml.</p>
        <p>Words in the Armenian transcription use lemma, the exact GREgORI pos code,
          lemmaRef for the lexical record, and contextual msd values documented in
          ../indices/listpos.xml. Nominal order is case.number, such as Nom.Pl; finite
          verbal order is tense-or-stem.mood.voice.person.number, such as
          Aor.Ind.Pass.3.Sg. Non-finite order is form.voice.case.number, such as
          Part.Dat.Sg. Record only applicable, established features, retaining their
          order. Pres.Sub and Aor.Sub distinguish stem formations, not temporal meaning.
          Tag each component of a compound verbal construction according to its own form.
          Non-finite forms receive no grammatical person. Explain unresolved analysis
          separately rather than treating it as inapplicable.</p>
        <p>Use persName/@ref, placeName/@ref, and title/@ref for identified entities in English. Collective designations use rs/@type="people" with a personGrp reference.</p>
        <p>Document production/post-production classification separately from witness
          transmission status and date. A copied production colophon remains a production
          colophon even though its surviving inscription is later. Dates narrated by the
          text do not automatically date the physical witness.</p>
        <p>Each surviving copy is encoded in a separate witness division, containing its own mise-en-page description, folio divisions, Armenian transcription, and corresponding English translation. The base witness is presented first; this does not imply that it preserves the original inscription.</p>
      </editorialDecl>
    </encodingDesc>
